#: conf.json:summary
msgid "Algebraic traits and default numeric instances that define the generic foundation for LunaFlow math packages."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/core.md:1
msgid "core API"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/core.md:3
#: manual/api/hom.md:3
msgid "Purpose"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/core.md:5
msgid "`luna-generic` is the algebraic trait layer of LunaFlow. It does not provide numerical algorithms or containers by itself; it defines the reusable capability surface that higher-level packages depend on."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/core.md:9
msgid "This page lists the traits, the conversion functions and the shipped instances. The certificates `Hom` and `Section` are on the [hom API](hom.md) page. The reasons behind the shapes are in the [core design](../design/core.md)."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/core.md:14
msgid "Importing"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/core.md:16
msgid "Add the package to your `moon.pkg`:"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/core.md:24
msgid "The examples on this page bring the names into scope with one `using` declaration, so they can be written without the `@lf_alg.` prefix:"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/core.md:57
msgid "Laws are contracts"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/core.md:59
msgid "Operation bundles (`AddMonoidOps`, `SemiringOps`, `RingOps`, `FieldOps`, and `NumOps`) describe available operations without asserting equations. Exact structural traits (`AddMonoid`, `Semiring`, `Ring`, `Field`, and the others) require a method returning an opaque `Law[Theory, Self]`. Implementations choose an explicit `Law::postulate()` assumption or derive a weaker law through a kernel-owned implication. The compiler checks that a certificate is returned, not that its equations are true."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/core.md:67
msgid "`Float` and `Double` implement operation bundles and `RoundedArithmetic`; they do not receive exact structural law instances."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/core.md:70
msgid "Operation traits"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/core.md:72
msgid "These traits live in `src/operation.mbt`. They name single operations and state no laws on their own; the structural traits give them their meaning."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/core.md:77
msgid "`Zero::zero()` returns the additive identity of `Self`."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/core.md:85
msgid "In an `AddMonoid` it must satisfy $0 + x = x + 0 = x$. Shipped for every default numeric type."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/core.md:90
msgid "`One::one()` returns the multiplicative identity of `Self`."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/core.md:98
msgid "In a `MulMonoid` it must satisfy $1 \\cdot x = x \\cdot 1 = x$. Shipped for every default numeric type."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/core.md:112
msgid "`Inverse::inv(x)` returns the multiplicative inverse $x^{-1}$."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/core.md:120
msgid "In a `MulGroup` or `Field` it must satisfy $x \\cdot x^{-1} = x^{-1} \\cdot x = 1$ for every $x$ that has an inverse. The shipped `Float` and `Double` instances abort with a message on `0`, rather than returning an infinity as `1.0 / 0.0` does."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/core.md:133
msgid "`Conjugate::conjugate(x)` returns the conjugate $\\overline{x}$."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/core.md:141
msgid "No instance is shipped. Implementors usually make it an involution that respects the arithmetic: $\\overline{\\overline{x}} = x$, $\\overline{x + y} = \\overline{x} + \\overline{y}$ and $\\overline{xy} = \\overline{y}\\,\\overline{x}$, which is $\\overline{x}\\,\\overline{y}$ when multiplication commutes. The trait does not enforce these laws."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/core.md:164
msgid "Structural traits"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/core.md:166
msgid "These traits live in `src/structure.mbt`. Each returns an opaque law certificate. The equations are contracts represented by that certificate; the compiler does not prove them."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/core.md:172
msgid "`AddMonoid` is a type with an associative `+` and the identity `0`."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/core.md:184
msgid "`+` is not required to commute here; every structure that builds on `AddMonoid` in this package (`Semiring`, `Ring`) requires it to."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/core.md:200
msgid "`MulMonoid` is a type with an associative `*` and the identity `1`."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/core.md:229
msgid "`AddGroup` is an `AddMonoid` where every element has a negative."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/core.md:241
msgid "Unsigned types do not implement it; see the semantic notes below."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/core.md:255
msgid "`MulGroup` is a `MulMonoid` with inverses and division."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/core.md:267
msgid "`Float` and `Double` implement `MulGroupOps` for executable operations, but they do not provide the exact `MulGroup` law certificate: `0` has no inverse and floating-point identities are rounded."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/core.md:273
msgid "`Semiring` is an exact law contract over semiring operations."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/core.md:286
msgid "Signed and unsigned integer types and `BigInt` provide an explicit `SemiringTh` assumption. `Float` and `Double` provide `SemiringOps` without a `Semiring` law instance."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/core.md:306
msgid "`Ring` is an exact law contract over ring operations. Its operation requirements are independent of its certificate."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/core.md:315
msgid "The `RingTh` certificate includes the ring axioms; multiplication need not commute. The compiler does not prove the equations represented by the returned certificate."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/core.md:331
msgid ""
"`Field` is a commutative, nontrivial field law contract over field operations: $0 \\n"
"e 1$, and every non-zero element has an inverse."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/core.md:340
msgid "An implementor must satisfy the `Ring` laws and, for all $a, b$:"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/core.md:347
msgid "Nontriviality and commutativity are part of the contract, as in the mathematical definition of a field. The compiler checks neither: the one-element zero ring satisfies the inverse law vacuously, and a type whose multiplication does not commute but has inverses (a division ring) also compiles as a `Field`. Generic code can then give wrong answers; for example, it may rewrite $(ab)^{-1}$ as $a^{-1}b^{-1}$. Do not implement `Field` for either type. The [core design](../design/core.md) explains the distinction."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/core.md:355
msgid "`Float` and `Double` provide field operations but no `FieldTh` certificate. Their `inv` aborts on `0`, while their `/` follows IEEE 754 and returns an infinity or NaN."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/core.md:377
msgid "`NumOps` provides absolute value and sign operations without claiming exact ring laws. The old `Num` trait is removed."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/core.md:387
msgid "`signum(x)` is $-1$, $0$ or $1$ according to the sign of $x$, and"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/core.md:393
msgid "Implemented by signed integers, `BigInt`, `Float` and `Double`. On a fixed-width type the identity fails at the most negative value, whose absolute value wraps back to itself. On `Float` and `Double`, `signum` returns its argument for `0.0`, `-0.0` and NaN. This operation bundle does not imply a `RingTh` law."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/core.md:406
msgid "Integer conversions"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/core.md:410
msgid "`FromNat::from_natural(n)` converts a non-negative `BigInt` value to `Self`. The operation alone does not certify that the conversion preserves semiring operations."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/core.md:420
msgid "Precondition: $n \\ge 0$. Exact preservation is a separate law claim; rounded conversions are ordinary operations only."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/core.md:423
msgid "Fixed-width integers reduce modulo $2^k$; `Float` and `Double` round to the nearest value, ties to even, as described under `FromInteger` below."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/core.md:428
msgid "`FromInteger::from_integer(n)` converts a `BigInt` value to `Self`. It does not construct a strict Hom certificate."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/core.md:437
msgid "It is defined for every integer, also on types without negation: on an unsigned type, `from_integer(-1)` is $2^k - 1$. Exact for `BigInt`, reduction modulo $2^k$ for fixed-width integers, rounding for `Float` and `Double`."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/core.md:442
msgid "For `Float` and `Double`, `from_integer(n)` is the IEEE 754 conversion convertFromInt: $n$ rounded to the nearest value with $p$ significant bits, $p = 24$ for `Float` and $p = 53$ for `Double`, ties to the even significand. Integers with $|n| \\le 2^p$ are exact. When the rounded value is larger than the largest finite number, the result is $\\pm\\infty$; the conversion never aborts:"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/core.md:449
msgid "Target"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/core.md:449
msgid "Exact up to"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/core.md:449
msgid "Result is $\\pm\\infty$ when"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/core.md:454
msgid "Zero gives $+0$. The [core design](../design/core.md) derives the rounding rule and the thresholds."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/core.md:482
msgid "To give your own number type these conversions, implement both traits; the [core tutorial](../tutorial/core.md) shows how."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/core.md:485
msgid "Integer types"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/core.md:489
msgid "`Integral` is an integer type: ℤ itself, or a quotient ℤ/2^k such as a fixed-width integer."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/core.md:498
msgid "`normalize(x)` returns the representative of `x` as a `BigInt`. The law is that `normalize` is a section of the canonical map:"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/core.md:505
msgid "The shipped instances pick $[-2^{k-1}, 2^{k-1})$ for signed types, $[0, 2^k)$ for unsigned ones, and the identity for `BigInt`. `normalize` is a homomorphism only for `BigInt`; on fixed-width types it is not, because their arithmetic wraps. `Section::of_integral` packages the pair as a certificate that can be checked."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/core.md:521
msgid "`Nat` is an `Integral` type whose representatives are non-negative: $\\operatorname{normalize}(x) \\ge 0$ for every `x`."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/core.md:529
msgid "Implemented by `UInt`, `UInt16` and `UInt64`. An arbitrary-precision ℕ is not a quotient of ℤ and therefore cannot be `Integral`; see the [core design](../design/core.md)."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/core.md:545
msgid "`lift_to(x)` lifts an integral value to its representative and maps it into any `FromInteger` target."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/core.md:552
msgid "It is `R::from_integer(x.normalize())`. It is a function, not a homomorphism: it converts the value `x` holds now, so a sum that has already wrapped in `S` stays wrapped. It preserves the operations exactly when the modulus of `R` divides that of `S`, as for `Int64 -> Int`; the [core design](../design/core.md) derives this condition."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/core.md:569
msgid "Shipped capabilities and laws"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/core.md:571
msgid "Type"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/core.md:571
msgid "Operation capabilities"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/core.md:571
msgid "Exact law instances"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/core.md:575
msgid "signed integer operation capabilities and `NumOps`"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/core.md:576
msgid "none"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/core.md:578
msgid "`Byte` implements none of them."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/core.md:580
#: manual/api/hom.md:789
msgid "Semantic notes"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/core.md:582
msgid "Fixed-width integers are ℤ/2^k. Their `from_integer` reduces modulo 2^k, and `normalize` picks the representative in the type's range: `[-2^(k-1), 2^(k-1))` for signed types, `[0, 2^k)` for unsigned ones."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/core.md:585
msgid "`BigInt` is ℤ itself: `from_integer` and `normalize` are the identity."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/core.md:586
msgid "`lift_to` is a homomorphism exactly when the target modulus divides the source modulus, as for `Int64 -> Int`. Into `BigInt`, `Float` or `Double` it is not one for fixed-width sources, because their arithmetic wraps."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/core.md:589
msgid "`Nat` covers the integral types whose representatives are non-negative. A true arbitrary-precision ℕ is not a quotient of ℤ and is out of scope."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/core.md:591
msgid "Unsigned integer instances stop at `Semiring`; they do not pretend to be additive groups or rings."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/core.md:593
msgid "`Float` and `Double` round to nearest, ties to even, in `from_natural` and `from_integer`, so very large values are approximate, and values beyond the largest finite number become $\\pm\\infty$ instead of aborting."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/core.md:596
msgid "A returned `Law[Th, Self]` is an explicit assumption; the compiler does not prove its equations. Float and Double do not receive exact field laws."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/core.md:599
msgid "Deprecated"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/core.md:603
msgid "`NatHomomorphism::from_nat(x)` lifts a `Nat` value to its representative and maps it into `Self`."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/core.md:612
msgid "Deprecated because the composite is not a homomorphism for fixed-width sources. Replacement: implement `FromNat` and `FromInteger`, and call `lift_to(x)`. Still implemented by `BigInt`, `Float` and `Double`."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/core.md:618
msgid "`IntegralHomomorphism::from_integral(x)` lifts an `Integral` value to its representative and maps it into `Self`."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/core.md:627
msgid "Deprecated for the same reason. Replacement: implement `FromInteger` and call `lift_to(x)`. Still implemented by `BigInt`, `Float` and `Double`."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/core.md:630
msgid "Additional exact structures and theory tags"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/core.md:632
msgid "`CommRing` requires `comm_ring_law() -> Law[CommRingTh, Self]` over `RingOps`: multiplication commutes. `IntegralDomain` requires `integral_domain_law() -> Law[IntegralDomainTh, Self]` over `RingOps`: it is a commutative ring with no zero divisors. Neither trait supplies a `FieldTh` law."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/core.md:637
msgid "`OrderedField` requires `ordered_field_law() -> Law[OrderedFieldTh, Self]` over `FieldOps + Compare`. Its order is total and compatible with addition and multiplication of nonnegative values. It supplies the precise premise needed for an exact Complex field instance in G3; `FieldTh` alone is insufficient."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/core.md:642
msgid "The public empty theory tags are `AddMonoidTh`, `MulMonoidTh`, `AddGroupTh`, `MulGroupTh`, `SemiringTh`, `RingTh`, `CommRingTh`, `IntegralDomainTh`, `FieldTh`, `OrderedFieldTh`, `IntegralTh`, `NatTh`, and `IntegerHomomorphismTh`. `IntegralTh` records the representative round trip; `NatTh` also records nonnegative representatives. `IntegerHomomorphismTh` records exact preservation by the BigInt conversion without injectivity."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/core.md:649
msgid "Law construction and kernel rules"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/core.md:651
msgid "`Law::postulate()` is the explicit trust entry for any theory and carrier. It does not prove equations. Weakening methods consume the stronger law and return only the indicated weaker law. Product methods consume both component laws and return the same theory on `Prod[A, B]`."
msgstr ""

