#: manual/api/hom.md:1
msgid "hom API"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/hom.md:5
msgid "The `hom` subsystem provides generalized homomorphisms. A `Hom[S, A, B]` is a map `A -> B` that carries a certificate stating it preserves every operation of the signature `S`. A `Section[S, Q, A]` certifies a lift that picks one representative per class of a quotient. Neither certificate can be forged outside this package. The unproven leaves are `Hom::postulate`, `Section::postulate`, `Law::postulate`, and `LawfulModel::postulate`. `Hom::from_integer_exact` consumes an explicit integer-homomorphism law; `Section::of_integral` obtains that law from `Integral`."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/hom.md:14
msgid "Source: `src/hom.mbt`, `src/section.mbt`. The mathematics is in the [hom design](../design/hom.md)."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/hom.md:17
msgid "The examples on this page assume this declaration:"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/hom.md:46
msgid "Signature tags"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/hom.md:48
msgid "Signature tags are empty enums that only appear at the type level, as the `S` parameter of `Hom`, `Section`, `Algebra` and `Reduct`. Users may define their own."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/hom.md:54
msgid "`AddMonoidSig` is the signature `0`, `+`."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/hom.md:63
msgid "`MulMonoidSig` is the signature `1`, `*`."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/hom.md:72
msgid "`AddGroupSig` is the signature `0`, `+`, `neg`."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/hom.md:81
msgid "`SemiringSig` is the signature `0`, `1`, `+`, `*`."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/hom.md:90
msgid "`RingSig` is the signature `0`, `1`, `+`, `*`, `neg`."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/hom.md:97
msgid "Algebra dictionaries"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/hom.md:99
msgid "Two dictionaries of the same `S` must list the same operations (name and arity) in the same order. `Algebra::prod` returns `Err(ModelMismatch)` when they do not. Finite `Hom::check` and `Hom::check_by` diagnostics return `false` for dictionaries with different operation signatures. These checks do not verify the Hom endpoint Model identities; `check_models` and `check_models_by` do that for the exact stored Models."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/hom.md:108
msgid "`Model[S, A]` binds an operation dictionary to one runtime identity. Two models with the same signature and carrier remain distinct when they were constructed separately, even if their operations happen to compare equal. The identity representation is opaque: callers cannot construct or mutate a `ModelKey`, and `Model::same_identity` is the only public identity operation."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/hom.md:128
msgid "Negative operation arity returns `Err(InvalidArity)` before allocating an identity. Successful custom Models use process-local `UInt64` identities. Construction is not thread-safe: callers must serialize it when using native threads. If the counter reaches `UInt64::max`, construction aborts with an internal-invariant failure before incrementing; it never wraps or reuses an identity. This exhaustion case is physically unreachable in supported use."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/hom.md:135
msgid "Canonical factories use one stable identity per signature and carrier type implementation. Repeated calls for the same canonical interpretation return Models with the same identity."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/hom.md:145
msgid "Construction errors and mismatch diagnostics"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/hom.md:147
msgid "`ModelConstructionError` reports invalid arity or an operation table that a kernel reduct cannot project. `ModelMismatch` reports different runtime model identities. Both derive `Eq` and `Debug`, so callers can compare errors or inspect their debug representation."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/hom.md:163
msgid "This canonical convenience binds the `AddGroupTh` certificate and canonical additive-group Model for `A`."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/hom.md:172
msgid "`Model::algebra()` returns the model's operation dictionary. `Model::prod` returns a fresh product identity with the two component identities recorded; reuse that value for pairing and projections. `Model::forget` projects a kernel mapping and returns `InvalidOperationTable` for incompatible tables."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/hom.md:183
msgid "`Algebra::make` and `Model::make` validate nonnegative arity only, even for built-in phantom signature tags. An empty table is allowed. Finite checks over empty operation lists return true vacuously and establish no law. `LawfulModel::postulate` can explicitly trust a rounded carrier; a small sample may pass while a larger one fails. The caller owns that assumption."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/hom.md:189
msgid "`ModelMismatch` reports incompatible identities, product components, or operation lists in checked composition and product construction. Both error types implement `Eq` and `Debug`; match their variants to handle failures."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/hom.md:195
msgid "`Op[A]` is one operation of a single-sorted signature."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/hom.md:205
msgid "`arity == 0` is a constant. `eval` receives exactly `arity` arguments."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/hom.md:209
msgid "`Algebra[S, A]` is an interpretation of the signature `S` on the carrier `A`: a list of `Op[A]` in the order the signature fixes."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/hom.md:220
msgid "`Algebra::make(ops)` builds a dictionary for a custom signature tag. It returns `Err(InvalidArity)` if any operation has a negative arity."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/hom.md:227
msgid "Keep one dictionary per tag and carrier, as explained above."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/hom.md:241
msgid "These functions derive the dictionary of a built-in tag from the structure trait instance of `A`."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/hom.md:252
msgid "The operations are listed in the order of the tag: for example `0`, `1`, `+`, `*`, `neg` for `RingSig`. Trait coherence gives one dictionary per tag and carrier."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/hom.md:267
msgid "`Algebra::prod(a, b)` is the componentwise product algebra on `Prod[A, B]`."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/hom.md:273
msgid "Each operation acts on `fst` with `a` and on `snd` with `b`. The actual signature returns `Result[Algebra[S, Prod[A, B]], ModelMismatch]` and rejects different operation lists without aborting."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/hom.md:277
msgid "Product type"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/hom.md:281
msgid "`Prod[A, B]` is the binary product carrier with fields `fst` and `snd`."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/hom.md:290
msgid "Operations act componentwise. `Prod` implements `Add`, `Mul`, `Neg`, `Sub`, `Zero` and `One`, and `AddMonoid`, `MulMonoid`, `AddGroup`, `Semiring` and `Ring` whenever both components do."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/hom.md:296
msgid "These methods are the componentwise operations, also available as `+`, `-`, `*`, unary `-` and the `Zero` / `One` traits."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/hom.md:315
msgid "These methods compare componentwise and render a `Prod` for debugging; use `==`, `!=` and `inspect` in new code."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/hom.md:333
msgid "Product law methods"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/hom.md:335
msgid "`Prod::add_monoid_law`, `Prod::mul_monoid_law`, `Prod::add_group_law`, `Prod::semiring_law`, and `Prod::ring_law` derive the corresponding law from both components. An operation-only component does not provide these methods."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/hom.md:347
msgid "Homomorphisms"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/hom.md:351
msgid "`Hom[S, A, B]` is a map `A -> B` certified to preserve every operation of `S`."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/hom.md:360
msgid "The certificate means that for every operation $\\omega$ of `S` with arity $n$ and all $x_1, \\dots, x_n$,"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/hom.md:367
msgid "Only this package constructs `Hom` values. The leaves are `Hom::postulate` and `Hom::from_integer_exact`; the latter requires a `LawfulModel[IntegerHomomorphismTh, SemiringSig, R]`. Every other constructor is an inference rule or a checked runtime transformation."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/hom.md:374
msgid "`Hom::postulate(source, target, f)` trusts `f` as an `S`-homomorphism without proof and creates a proof obligation. The two Models bind the operation interpretations used by the certificate."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/hom.md:382
msgid "The caller promises that for every operation `op` of `S` and all arguments `xs`, `f(op_A(xs)) == op_B(xs.map(f))`. The promise is always strict equality, even when the map is only checked with a lax or tolerance relation. Back every call with a `check` test."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/hom.md:399
msgid "`h.apply(x)` applies the underlying map."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/hom.md:413
msgid "`Hom::id(model)` is the identity homomorphism bound to the supplied runtime model. Both endpoints use that exact model identity."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/hom.md:422
msgid "`h.then_checked(g)` is the composite when the middle Model identities match. It returns `Err(ModelMismatch)` for different runtime interpretations."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/hom.md:429
msgid "If $f$ and $g$ preserve $\\omega$, so does $g \\circ f$:"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/hom.md:437
msgid "`h.forget(r)` forgets structure along a `Reduct[S, T]` witness."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/hom.md:443
msgid "A map that preserves every operation of `S` preserves the operations of the smaller signature `T`."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/hom.md:448
msgid "`Hom::pair(product, f, g)` maps `x` to `{ fst: f(x), snd: g(x) }`. The explicit product Model must be reused by `Hom::fst(product, left)` and `Hom::snd(product, right)`."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/hom.md:458
msgid "With the same product Model and component Models, the checked composites `Hom::pair(product, f, g).then_checked(Hom::fst(product, left))` and `Hom::pair(product, f, g).then_checked(Hom::snd(product, right))` return `f` and `g` respectively."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/hom.md:480
msgid "`h.to_add_group(source_law, target_law)` upgrades an additive monoid homomorphism after checking the law-bound Models match its endpoints."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/hom.md:487
msgid "No new obligation: $f(-x) + f(x) = f(-x + x) = f(0) = 0$, so $f(-x) = -f(x)$."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/hom.md:491
msgid "`h.to_ring(source_law, target_law)` upgrades a semiring homomorphism after checking the law-bound Models match its endpoints."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/hom.md:498
msgid "No new obligation, by the same computation as `to_add_group`."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/hom.md:502
msgid "`Hom::from_integer_exact(target)` is the canonical map ℤ → `R` only when the target Model carries an explicit `IntegerHomomorphismTh` law. This law states preservation of zero, one, addition and multiplication; it does not assert injectivity."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/hom.md:511
msgid "Rounded `Float` and `Double` conversions do not provide this law. A caller may use `Law::postulate` explicitly, but that assumption is visible in the type and trust audit. For a fixed-width source, use `Section::of_integral`."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/hom.md:525
#: manual/design/hom.md:50
msgid "Sections"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/hom.md:527
msgid "A section lifts a quotient `Q` back into `A` along a homomorphism `proj : A -> Q`, with `proj(lift(q)) == q`. The lift is not a homomorphism, but it preserves every operation up to the kernel of `proj`, and exactly whenever the result is itself a representative."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/hom.md:532
msgid "The section law rejects lifts that leave the class of their argument. It does not choose among representatives: `[0, 2^32)` and `[-2^31, 2^31)` are both sections of `BigInt -> Int`, and only the second preserves the signed order."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/hom.md:539
msgid "`Section[S, Q, A]` is a lift of the quotient `Q` back into `A` along the projection `proj : A -> Q`."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/hom.md:554
msgid "`Section::postulate(proj, lift)` trusts `lift` as a section of `proj` and creates a proof obligation."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/hom.md:561
msgid "The caller promises `proj.apply(lift(q)) == q` for every `q`; `proj` carries its own `Hom` obligation."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/hom.md:576
msgid "`Section::of_integral()` is the canonical section of an integral type."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/hom.md:582
msgid "`proj` is `FromInteger::from_integer` and `lift` is `Integral::normalize`. The obligation sits on those instances. Use `to_ring` when `Z` is a ring."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/hom.md:587
msgid "`s.lift(q)` is the chosen representative of `q`; `s.proj()` is the projection onto the quotient."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/hom.md:597
msgid "`s.normalize(a)` is `lift(proj(a))`, the normal form of `a`."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/hom.md:603
msgid "Two values are congruent exactly when their normal forms are equal."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/hom.md:607
msgid "`s.is_representative(a)` tells whether `a` is the chosen representative of its class, that is `normalize(a) == a`."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/hom.md:614
msgid "On such results the lift agrees exactly with the operations of `A`."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/hom.md:632
msgid "`s.then(next)` lifts `Q` into `A` with `s`, then `A` into `B` with `next`."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/hom.md:638
msgid "The projection is `next.proj` followed by `s.proj`. No new obligation: with $s, t$ the two lifts and $\\pi_s, \\pi_t$ their projections, $\\pi_s(\\pi_t(t(s(q)))) = \\pi_s(s(q)) = q$."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/hom.md:644
msgid "These inference rules change the signature of the projection; the lift is unchanged."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/hom.md:653
msgid "They follow `Hom::forget`, `Hom::to_add_group` and `Hom::to_ring`."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/hom.md:657
msgid "`s.check(samples)` tests the section law `proj(lift(q)) == q` on every sample."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/hom.md:666
msgid "`s.check_ops(quotient, cover, samples)` tests `proj(op_A(xs.map(lift))) == op_Q(xs)` for every operation over all tuples drawn from `samples`."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/hom.md:674
msgid "This exercises `proj` as a homomorphism on representatives. It returns `false` when the two dictionaries do not list the same operations."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/hom.md:687
msgid "`lift_to(x)` lifts an integral value and maps it into any `FromInteger` target without a certificate; it is listed on the [core API](core.md)."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/hom.md:690
msgid "Reduct witnesses"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/hom.md:694
msgid "`Reduct[S, T]` witnesses that every `S`-structure is also a `T`-structure, so an `S`-homomorphism is also a `T`-homomorphism."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/hom.md:703
msgid "Only this package constructs `Reduct` values."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/hom.md:707
msgid "These constants are the inclusions between the built-in signatures."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/hom.md:719
msgid "`Reduct::refl()` is the trivial inclusion of `S` in itself; `r.then(r2)` composes inclusions."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/hom.md:735
msgid "Law checks"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/hom.md:739
msgid "`h.check(src, dst, samples)` tests the homomorphism law with exact equality on every operation of `S`."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/hom.md:746
msgid "Requires `B : Eq`. It is `check_by` with `==`. Both Algebra-only diagnostics test only the supplied tables: they do not verify the Hom endpoint Model identities. Use `check_models` or `check_models_by` to validate those identities."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/hom.md:752
msgid "`h.check_by(src, dst, samples, rel)` tests `rel(f(op_A(xs)), op_B(f(xs)))` for every operation over all tuples drawn from `samples`."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/hom.md:759
msgid "`rel` sets the strength of preservation: equality for strict homomorphisms, `<=` for lax ones such as subadditive maps, and a tolerance for approximate homomorphisms into floating-point targets. An operation of arity `n` is tested on `samples.length()^n` tuples. It returns `false` when `src` and `dst` do not list the same operations."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/hom.md:767
msgid "These diagnostics bind the finite check to the exact source and target Models stored in the Hom. They return `Err(ModelMismatch)` when either endpoint has a different runtime identity, then delegate to `check` or `check_by`. They still return diagnostics only and never create a certificate."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/hom.md:791
msgid "Fixed-width integer sources (`Int`, `Int64`, `UInt`, ...) are ℤ/2^k rather than the integers. There is no semiring map from them into `BigInt`, so a lift into ℤ is a section, not a homomorphism. The same argument rules out widening such as `Int -> Int64`, while truncation such as `Int64 -> Int` is a ring homomorphism."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/hom.md:796
msgid "The inference rules compose certificates as strict homomorphisms. A map that only satisfies a lax (`<=`) or tolerance law must be checked again after composition."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/hom.md:799
msgid "`Float` and `Double` provide rounded operations without exact law certificates; use `check_by` with a tolerance for approximate maps. See the embedding notes in the [core API](core.md)."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/hom.md:803
msgid "Removed conversion constructors"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/hom.md:805
msgid "`Hom::from_nat`, `Hom::from_integral` and the old `Hom::from_integer` are not part of the 0.5.0 API. Use `FromNat` / `FromInteger` for ordinary values, `Section::of_integral` for representative lifts, and `Hom::from_integer_exact` only with an explicit integer-homomorphism law."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/core.md:1
msgid "core design"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/core.md:3
msgid "Design goal"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/core.md:5
msgid "`luna-generic` gives LunaFlow a shared algebraic vocabulary. Packages such as `arithmetic`, `luna-complex`, `linear-algebra`, and `luna-poly` should be able to describe their requirements in terms of reusable traits instead of inventing their own incompatible capability layers."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/core.md:10
msgid "Main design decisions"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/core.md:12
msgid "The operation capability graph and the exact law layer are separate. Bundles such as `RingOps` and `FieldOps` compose primitive operations; exact structures return opaque `Law[Theory, Self]` certificates."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/core.md:15
msgid "Structural traits such as `Ring`, `Field`, `Integral`, and `Nat` do not inherit exact laws through a supertrait chain. Each law has a distinct theory tag and an explicit certificate method."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/core.md:18
msgid "Conversions are split into two halves that meet at ℤ, represented by `BigInt`. The target side is an operation `FromInteger`; it does not by itself claim to be a homomorphism. The source side is `Integral::normalize`, which picks a representative. `lift_to` composes the operations and promises nothing about preservation."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/core.md:23
msgid "The halves are single-parameter traits because every conversion factors through ℤ, the initial ring: no trait has to relate two types."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/core.md:25
msgid "`Integral` combines semiring operations and `FromInteger`, and returns an explicit `IntegralTh` certificate for `from_integer(normalize(x)) == x`. This makes an integral type a quotient of ℤ with chosen representatives. `FromInteger` takes a `BigInt` rather than a generic integral source, so the traits do not refer to each other."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/core.md:30
msgid "Unsigned types stop before additive inverse, so the abstraction stays mathematically honest."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/core.md:32
msgid "`Field` means a commutative field. `FieldTh` is a contract returned by the implementor; neither the compiler nor finite tests prove its equations."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/core.md:35
msgid "Mathematical background"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/core.md:37
msgid "Operation bundles state which operations are available. Exact structure certificates state which equations are assumed. With $x, y, z$ ranging over the carrier:"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/core.md:41
msgid "Law trait"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/core.md:41
msgid "Theory tag"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/core.md:41
msgid "Equations represented"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/core.md:46
msgid "inverse laws for nonzero elements"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/core.md:47
msgid "distributivity, additive commutativity, and zero absorption"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/core.md:48
msgid "ring laws, including additive inverses"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/core.md:49
msgid "ring laws and commutative multiplication"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/core.md:50
msgid "commutative ring laws and no zero divisors"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/core.md:51
msgid ""
"$0 \\n"
"e 1$, $xy = yx$, inverse and division laws"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/core.md:52
msgid "ordered-field laws"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/core.md:54
msgid "The operation bundle graph describes available methods only. Kernel-owned weakening functions expose proven implications between law tags, such as `FieldTh -> RingTh` and `RingTh -> SemiringTh`; there is no `RingTh -> CommRingTh` rule. A generic bound on operations does not imply any exact equations."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/core.md:60
msgid "A homomorphism $\\varphi : A \\to B$ of such structures preserves every operation of the signature:"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/core.md:69
msgid "Integers as quotients of ℤ"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/core.md:71
msgid "This section gives the mathematics behind `FromNat`, `FromInteger`, `Integral` and `lift_to`. The [core tutorial](../tutorial/core.md) shows how to use them without it."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/core.md:75
msgid "The canonical map is unique"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/core.md:77
msgid "For every ring `R` there is exactly one ring homomorphism `ℤ -> R`. A ring homomorphism `φ` must send `1` to `1`, and additivity then forces `φ(n) = 1 + ... + 1` (`n` times) for `n > 0` and `φ(-n) = -φ(n)`. Conversely, `n ↦ n·1` preserves `+` and `*` by distributivity. The same argument gives exactly one semiring homomorphism `ℕ -> R` for every semiring `R`. In categorical terms ℕ and ℤ are initial objects."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/core.md:84
msgid "The derivation in full, first for ℕ. Let $R$ be a semiring and define $\\varphi : \\mathbb{N} \\to R$ by recursion:"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/core.md:91
msgid "Additivity, by induction on $n$ (associativity of $+$ in $R$):"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/core.md:101
msgid "Multiplicativity, by induction on $n$ (absorption $x \\cdot 0 = 0$, additivity, distributivity):"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/core.md:112
msgid "Uniqueness: any semiring homomorphism $\\psi$ satisfies $\\psi(0) = 0$ and $\\psi(n + 1) = \\psi(n) + \\psi(1) = \\psi(n) + 1$, the same recursion, so $\\psi = \\varphi$ by induction."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/core.md:116
msgid "For ℤ, let $R$ be a ring. Every integer is a difference $a - b$ of naturals; put $\\hat\\varphi(a - b) = \\varphi(a) - \\varphi(b)$. This is well defined, because $a - b = c - d$ means $a + d = b + c$ in ℕ, hence $\\varphi(a) + \\varphi(d) = \\varphi(b) + \\varphi(c)$ in $R$, and adding $-\\varphi(b) - \\varphi(d)$ to both sides (with $+$ commutative) gives $\\varphi(a) - \\varphi(b) = \\varphi(c) - \\varphi(d)$. It is additive term by term, and multiplicative by distributivity:"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/core.md:132
msgid "A ring homomorphism must also satisfy $\\psi(-n) = -\\psi(n)$, so it agrees with $\\hat\\varphi$ on negatives too, and $\\hat\\varphi$ is unique.[^initial]"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/core.md:135
msgid "In the language of category theory, ℕ is the initial object of the category of semirings and ℤ that of rings. ℤ is the Grothendieck group of the additive monoid ℕ, which is the construction used above."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/core.md:139
msgid "Because the mathematical map depends only on `R`, its operation can use a single-parameter trait: `FromNat::from_natural` and `FromInteger::from_integer`. These conversion operations do not themselves carry the exact homomorphism law; a strict certificate needs an explicit target-bound law premise."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/core.md:145
msgid "Fixed-width integers"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/core.md:147
msgid "`Int` adds and multiplies modulo 2^32, so as a ring it is ℤ/2^32. Its `from_integer` is the reduction `ℤ -> ℤ/2^32`, which is surjective. `normalize` goes the other way and picks one integer in every residue class, the one in `[-2^31, 2^31)`. The law `from_integer(normalize(x)) == x` says exactly that `normalize` is a section of the reduction:"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/core.md:153
msgid "`normalize` is injective, because it has a left inverse."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/core.md:154
msgid "Its image contains exactly one element of every class: at most one because it is injective, and at least one because of the law."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/core.md:156
msgid "It is not a homomorphism: `normalize(2147483647 + 1) = -2^31`, while `normalize(2147483647) + normalize(1) = 2^31`."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/core.md:159
msgid "In symbols, with $m = 2^k$ and $\\pi : \\mathbb{Z} \\to \\mathbb{Z}/m$ the reduction, whose kernel is $m\\mathbb{Z}$, the shipped instances use"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/core.md:168
msgid "with $\\bmod$ taking values in $[0, 2^k)$. Both are well defined, since $n$ and $n + jm$ give the same value, and both satisfy $\\pi(s(x)) = x$, since each differs from $n$ by a multiple of $m$. The two injectivity steps above, written out:"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/core.md:177
msgid "The failure of additivity is a multiple of the modulus:"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/core.md:185
msgid "For $x = 2^{31} - 1$ and $y = 1$ on `Int` the difference is $2^{32}$, so no choice of representatives can repair it: $s$ is a homomorphism only when $m = 0$, that is for `BigInt`."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/core.md:189
msgid "The [hom design](hom.md) shows what a section does preserve."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/core.md:191
msgid "When a conversion is a homomorphism"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/core.md:193
msgid "`lift_to : S -> R` is `R::from_integer` composed with `S::normalize`. When `S` is ℤ/m (with `m = 0` for `BigInt`), a ring homomorphism `ℤ/m -> R` exists if and only if `m·1 = 0` in `R`:"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/core.md:197
msgid "If `ψ` is one, then `0 = ψ(0) = ψ(m·1) = m·1` in `R`."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/core.md:198
msgid "If `m·1 = 0`, the canonical map `ℤ -> R` sends `mℤ` to `0` and so factors through ℤ/m. It is unique because `ℤ -> ℤ/m` is surjective."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/core.md:201
msgid "In that case `lift_to` is this homomorphism: write the canonical map `ℤ -> R` as `ψ ∘ π` with `π : ℤ -> ℤ/m`; then `lift_to = ψ ∘ π ∘ normalize = ψ`. So:"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/core.md:205
msgid "`Int64 -> Int` is a homomorphism, because `2^64 ≡ 0 (mod 2^32)`."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/core.md:206
msgid "`Int -> Int64` is not, because `2^32 ≢ 0 (mod 2^64)`."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/core.md:207
msgid "No fixed-width integer maps homomorphically into `BigInt`, `Float` or `Double`, where `m·1 ≠ 0` for every `m > 0`."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/core.md:210
msgid "The same argument as one chain, with $\\iota_R : \\mathbb{Z} \\to R$ the canonical map and $\\iota_R = \\psi \\circ \\pi$ when $m \\cdot 1_R = 0$:"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/core.md:218
msgid ""
"For the two modulus examples, $2^{64} \\cdot 1 = 2^{32} \\cdot (2^{32} \\cdot 1) = 2^{32} \\cdot 0 = 0$ in ℤ/2^32, while $2^{32} \\cdot 1 \\n"
"eq 0$ in ℤ/2^64 because $0 < 2^{32} < 2^{64}$."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/core.md:222
msgid "Why unsigned types stop at `Semiring`"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/core.md:224
msgid "`UInt`, `UInt16` and `UInt64` are read as natural numbers that wrap: `Nat` promises non-negative representatives, and code over them treats values as counts and sizes. ℕ has no additive inverses, so it is a semiring and not a ring:"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/core.md:233
msgid "a contradiction. As an abstract ring the unsigned type is ℤ/2^k, which does have negatives, $-x = 2^k - x$. Exposing them would make `-1` silently mean $2^k - 1$ in generic code written for rings, which is the confusion the `Nat` reading avoids. The core library also provides no `Neg` for unsigned types, and MoonBit does not let this package add an instance of a foreign trait to a foreign type, so `AddGroup` could not be implemented without a wrapper type anyway. The canonical map is still total: `from_integer(-1)` on `UInt` is $2^{32} - 1$, the image of $-1$ in ℤ/2^32."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/core.md:242
msgid "Why the traits do not refer to each other"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/core.md:244
msgid "Every conversion factors through ℤ: `S -> ℤ -> R`. The first half depends only on `S` (`Integral`), the second only on `R` (`FromInteger`), so neither trait has to relate two types. `FromInteger` takes a `BigInt` rather than a generic integral source, which lets `Integral` extend it without a cycle."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/core.md:249
msgid "Fields and division rings"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/core.md:251
msgid "`Field` is `Ring + Inverse + Div` with commutative multiplication. The commutativity is part of the contract even though no method states it."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/core.md:254
msgid "Definitions"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/core.md:256
msgid ""
"A *division ring* is a ring with $1 \\n"
"eq 0$ in which every $a \\n"
"eq 0$ has a two-sided inverse, $a a^{-1} = a^{-1} a = 1$. A *field* is a division ring whose multiplication commutes, $ab = ba$. The two differ only in that law, and the method signatures of `Ring + Inverse + Div` cannot tell them apart."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/core.md:261
msgid "The standard example of a division ring that is not a field is Hamilton's quaternions ℍ, with basis $1, i, j, k$ and $i^2 = j^2 = k^2 = ijk = -1$. From $ijk = -1$, multiplying on the right by $k$ gives $ij \\cdot k^2 = -k$, so $ij = k$; similarly $ji = -k$:"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/core.md:270
msgid "What breaks without commutativity"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/core.md:272
msgid "In any division ring the inverse of a product reverses the order:"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/core.md:279
msgid "The other order is the inverse of the other product, $a^{-1}b^{-1} = (ba)^{-1}$, and inversion is injective, so"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/core.md:286
msgid "In ℍ, $(ij)^{-1} = k^{-1} = -k$, while $i^{-1} j^{-1} = (-i)(-j) = ij = k$. Division is ambiguous in the same way: $a b^{-1}$ and $b^{-1} a$ are different elements in general, and `Div` provides only one of them."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/core.md:291
msgid "Generic code bounded by `F : Field` may rely on $ab = ba$: rewrite $(ab)^{-1}$ as $a^{-1}b^{-1}$, compute $a/b \\cdot c$ as $ac/b$, or reorder products to save work. A non-commutative type that implemented `Field` would compile and then get wrong answers from such code. That is why the trait states commutativity, and why a division ring that is not a field must not implement it. Generic code that also works for division rings asks for `Ring + Inverse + Div` and keeps the order of its factors."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/core.md:299
msgid "Every finite division ring is commutative,[^wedderburn] so the distinction only arises for infinite types."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/core.md:302
msgid "Wedderburn's little theorem (1905): a finite division ring is a field. Over the reals, Frobenius' theorem (1877) adds that the only finite-dimensional associative division algebras are ℝ, ℂ and ℍ."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/core.md:306
msgid "Floating-point operation instances"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/core.md:308
msgid "`Float` and `Double` implement field operations and the `RoundedArithmetic` marker. They have no exact `FieldTh` certificate: associativity and distributivity hold only approximately, and `0` has no inverse. Their `inv` operation aborts on zero."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/core.md:313
msgid "Rounding ℤ into `Float` and `Double`"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/core.md:315
msgid "A binary floating-point format with precision $p$ and maximum exponent $e_{\\max}$ has the positive finite values"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/core.md:322
msgid "plus subnormals, which no non-zero integer needs because $e \\ge 0$ for $|n| \\ge 1$. `Double` has $p = 53$, $e_{\\max} = 1023$; `Float` has $p = 24$, $e_{\\max} = 127$. No map ℤ → `Double` is a ring homomorphism, since $2^{53} + 1$ is not representable, so `from_integer` is the next best thing: the IEEE 754-2019 conversion convertFromInt (§5.4.1) with the default rounding attribute roundTiesToEven (§4.3.1). Write $\\mathrm{RN}_p$ for it. It is odd, $\\mathrm{RN}_p(-n) = -\\mathrm{RN}_p(n)$, monotone, exact for $|n| \\le 2^p$, and its relative error is at most $2^{-p}$ while the result is finite."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/core.md:332
msgid "**The rounding step.** Let $m = |n| > 0$ with $2^{n_b - 1} \\le m < 2^{n_b}$, so $n_b$ is the bit length of $m$, and set $s = n_b - p - 1$. When $s \\ge 0$, divide with remainder,"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/core.md:340
msgid "when $s < 0$, put $t = m \\cdot 2^{-s}$ and $r = 0$. Either way $2^p \\le t < 2^{p+1}$: $t$ holds exactly the top $p + 1$ bits of $m$. Split off its last bit, $t = 2q + b$ with $b \\in \\{0, 1\\}$, and"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/core.md:349
msgid "The two neighbours of $m$ with $p$ significant bits are $q \\cdot 2^{s+1}$ and $(q + 1) \\cdot 2^{s+1}$, and round-to-nearest-even takes the upper one exactly when $f > \\tfrac12$, or $f = \\tfrac12$ and $q$ is odd. Because $r / 2^{s+1} < \\tfrac12$,"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/core.md:359
msgid ""
"so the rule needs only the round bit $b$, the sticky bit $[r \\n"
"eq 0]$ and the parity of $q$:"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/core.md:370
msgid "The exponent is $e = (p - 1) + (s + 1) = n_b - 1$. If rounding up carries, $q + 1 = 2^p$, the value is $2^{p-1} \\cdot 2^{s+2}$ and $e = n_b$. The implementation computes $t$ as `m >> s`, the sticky bit as `(t << s) != m`, and assembles the result from its bit pattern: sign, biased exponent $e + e_{\\max}$, and $q$ without its leading bit. It uses only `bit_length`, shifts, `to_uint64` and comparisons of `BigInt`, which behave identically on every backend, and it runs in time linear in the length of $n$."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/core.md:379
msgid "**Overflow.** The largest finite value is"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/core.md:385
msgid "Its significand $2^p - 1$ is odd, so the midpoint between $\\Omega$ and $2^{e_{\\max}+1}$, namely $2^{e_{\\max}+1} - 2^{e_{\\max}-p}$, rounds up to $2^{e_{\\max}+1}$, which does not fit. Following §7.4, an overflowing result under roundTiesToEven is $\\pm\\infty$ with the sign of $n$:"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/core.md:394
msgid "that is $|n| \\ge 2^{1024} - 2^{970}$ for `Double` and $|n| \\ge 2^{128} - 2^{103}$ for `Float`. The integer just below each threshold still rounds to $\\Omega$."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/core.md:398
msgid "**Why `Float` is rounded once.** Rounding to `Double` and then to `Float` is not $\\mathrm{RN}_{24}$. Take $n = 2^{53} + 2^{29} + 1$. At 53 bits the spacing is $2$, so $n$ is halfway between $2^{53} + 2^{29}$ and $2^{53} + 2^{29} + 2$, and the tie goes to the even significand, $2^{53} + 2^{29}$. That value is exactly halfway between the `Float` neighbours $2^{53}$ and $2^{53} + 2^{30}$, and the second rounding goes to the even one, $2^{53}$. But $n$ itself lies above that midpoint, so $\\mathrm{RN}_{24}(n) = 2^{53} + 2^{30}$. `Float` therefore runs the same rounding step with $p = 24$ directly."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/core.md:408
msgid "**Why not through a decimal string.** Earlier versions printed the `BigInt` in decimal and parsed the result as a `Double`. That is correctly rounded in range, but the parser rejects values at or above the overflow threshold, so the conversion aborted instead of returning $\\pm\\infty$; the decimal conversion costs time quadratic in the length on some backends; and `Float` was rounded twice."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/core.md:415
#: manual/design/hom.md:154
msgid "Alternatives rejected"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/core.md:417
msgid "A two-parameter conversion trait `Into[S, R]`: MoonBit traits have only `Self`, and the factorization through ℤ makes it unnecessary."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/core.md:419
msgid "One broad \"number\" trait: it would hide the difference between ℤ, ℤ/2^k, approximate reals and fields, which are exactly the differences generic code has to respect."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/core.md:422
msgid "`NatHomomorphism` and `IntegralHomomorphism` as the conversion interface: they promised a homomorphism that a fixed-width source cannot give. They remain only as deprecated traits."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/core.md:425
msgid "A separate division-ring trait: no shipped type needs it, and code that must work without commutativity can ask for `Ring + Inverse + Div`."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/core.md:428
#: manual/design/hom.md:170
msgid "Boundaries"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/core.md:430
msgid "This package does not define matrices, complex numbers, polynomials, parsing, or numerical algorithms."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/core.md:432
msgid "It does not erase the semantic difference between exact and approximate number systems."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/core.md:434
msgid "It does not check laws at compile time. The laws of every structural trait, including the commutativity of `Field`, are contracts on the implementor, tested with the tools of the [hom API](../api/hom.md)."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/core.md:437
msgid "It does not model an arbitrary-precision ℕ: such a type is not a quotient of ℤ, so it cannot be `Integral`."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/hom.md:1
msgid "hom design"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/hom.md:3
msgid "Goals"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/hom.md:5
msgid "Express structure-preserving maps within MoonBit's current type system, hand the laws the type system cannot prove to the developer, and keep those obligations auditable and testable."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/hom.md:9
msgid "Constraints"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/hom.md:11
msgid "MoonBit traits only have the `Self` parameter: there are no multi-parameter traits and no associated types, so a homomorphism `A -> B` cannot be a trait."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/hom.md:13
msgid "Homomorphisms out of ℕ and ℤ are unique (initial objects), which is why `FromNat` and `FromInteger` can live as target-side traits. Other homomorphisms are generally not unique and must be values."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/hom.md:17
msgid "Core decisions"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/hom.md:19
msgid "LCF-style certificates: `Hom[S, A, B]` has private fields and is only constructed inside this package."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/hom.md:21
msgid "`Hom::postulate`, `Section::postulate`, `Law::postulate` and `LawfulModel::postulate` are explicit trust entries. Kernel rules use the package-private `trust`; searching for `postulate` therefore exposes every user-level assumption. `Hom::from_integer_exact` requires an opaque `IntegerHomomorphismTh` bound to its target Model, while `Section::of_integral` obtains that law from `Integral`."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/hom.md:27
msgid "A lift from a quotient back to its cover is a `Section`, not a `Hom`. It carries the projection as a `Hom` and only promises `proj(lift(q)) == q`; agreement with the operations on representatives follows from that law. Keeping the two apart stops a representative lift, such as `Int -> BigInt`, from being composed as if it were a homomorphism."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/hom.md:32
msgid "The signature is a phantom type `S` on the certificate, while strict `Hom` values carry source and target `Model` identities. The model binds the operation table to the certificate; `then_checked`, pairing and projections return `ModelMismatch` instead of silently composing different interpretations. Algebra-only `check` and `check_by` remain finite diagnostics and never create certificates; `check_models` and `check_models_by` add endpoint identity validation before delegating to those finite checks."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/hom.md:40
msgid "Custom operations supplied to `Algebra::make` must have nonnegative arity. Law checking enumerates argument tuples recursively, so rejecting invalid arities at construction prevents nontermination before a certificate is checked."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/hom.md:44
msgid "Signature inclusions are `Reduct[S, T]` witnesses only this package can build. Built-in reducts project operation tables and preserve custom lineage; `Reduct::refl` leaves the table and identity unchanged."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/hom.md:47
msgid "The strength of preservation is the relation `rel` chosen at check time, so strict, lax and approximate homomorphisms share one API."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/hom.md:52
msgid "The [hom tutorial](../tutorial/hom.md) uses `Section` without this mathematics."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/hom.md:55
msgid "Definition"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/hom.md:57
msgid "Let `π : A -> Q` be a surjective homomorphism, for example the reduction `BigInt -> Int`. A section is a map `s : Q -> A` with `π(s(q)) = q` for every `q`: it picks one element of every class `π⁻¹(q)`. By the first isomorphism theorem `Q` is the quotient `A / ker π`, so a section is a choice of representatives for a quotient algebra."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/hom.md:63
msgid "What a section preserves"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/hom.md:65
msgid "For every operation `ω` and arguments `x`:"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/hom.md:67
msgid "`s(ω(x))` and `ω(s(x))` are congruent modulo `ker π`. Apply `π` to both: `π(s(ω(x))) = ω(x)` by the section law, and `π(ω(s(x))) = ω(π(s(x))) = ω(x)` because `π` is a homomorphism."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/hom.md:70
msgid "`s(ω(x)) = ω(s(x))` exactly when `ω(s(x))` lies in the image of `s`. If `ω(s(x)) = s(y)`, then `y = π(s(y)) = π(ω(s(x))) = ω(x)` by the same computation, so `ω(s(x)) = s(ω(x))`. Conversely `s(ω(x))` is always in the image."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/hom.md:75
msgid "The same two steps in display form, for an $n$-ary operation $\\omega$ and $x = (x_1, \\dots, x_n)$ with $s(x) = (s(x_1), \\dots, s(x_n))$:"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/hom.md:87
msgid "so $s(\\omega_Q(x)) - \\omega_A(s(x)) \\in \\ker \\pi$ whenever $A$ has subtraction. If $\\omega_A(s(x)) = s(y)$ for some $y$, applying $\\pi$ gives $y = \\omega_Q(x)$, hence $\\omega_A(s(x)) = s(\\omega_Q(x))$."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/hom.md:91
msgid "So `Section::check` only tests the section law; `check_ops` tests that `π` is a homomorphism on lifted arguments, and point 2 follows. For `Int`, the image of `s` is `[-2^31, 2^31)`, and \"the result is in the image\" means \"the result did not wrap around\"."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/hom.md:96
msgid "The carry"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/hom.md:98
msgid "For addition on `Int` the difference in point 1 is `s(a) + s(b) - s(a + b) = c(a, b)·2^32` with `c(a, b) ∈ {-1, 0, 1}`, the carry. Expanding `s(a) + s(b) + s(e)` in two ways gives"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/hom.md:106
msgid "The identity comes from associativity. Write $m = 2^{32}$ and $s(a) + s(b) = s(a + b) + c(a, b)\\,m$, then group the sum of three lifts both ways:"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/hom.md:119
msgid "Both sides are equal in ℤ, so the coefficients of $m$ agree. The bound on $c$ follows from the range of the representatives: $s(a) + s(b) \\in [-2^{32}, 2^{32} - 2]$ and $s(a + b) \\in [-2^{31}, 2^{31})$, so $c(a, b)\\,m$ lies strictly between $-3 \\cdot 2^{31}$ and $3 \\cdot 2^{31}$, and $c(a, b) \\in \\{-1, 0, 1\\}$."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/hom.md:125
msgid "So `c` is a 2-cocycle describing ℤ as an extension of ℤ/2^32 by 2^32ℤ. That extension does not split, because ℤ has no element of finite order, so no choice of representatives makes `s` a homomorphism. Directly: an additive section $\\sigma : \\mathbb{Z}/m \\to \\mathbb{Z}$ would give $m\\,\\sigma(1) = \\sigma(m \\cdot 1) = \\sigma(0) = 0$, so $\\sigma(1) = 0$, contradicting $\\pi(\\sigma(1)) = 1$. For multiplication the difference is the high word of the product."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/hom.md:133
msgid "Normal forms"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/hom.md:135
msgid "`n = s ∘ π : A -> A` is a normal form: `n(n(a)) = n(a)`, `a` and `n(a)` are congruent, and `a`, `b` are congruent exactly when `n(a) = n(b)`. The quotient operations are computed on representatives as `s(ω_Q(x)) = n(ω_A(s(x)))`: compute in `A`, then normalize. Wrapped `Int` arithmetic is this computation for `A = ℤ`. Because `Section::normalize` is built from `s` and `π`, it cannot give two congruent values different normal forms."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/hom.md:143
msgid "Why `Section` is not a `Hom`"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/hom.md:145
msgid "A section is injective and agrees with the operations on its image, so it is easy to mistake for a homomorphism and compose it as one. Keeping it in a separate certificate makes the difference visible in the types: `Section` carries `π` as a `Hom`, and only promises `π(s(q)) = q`."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/hom.md:150
msgid "The law does not fix which representatives are chosen. `[0, 2^32)` and `[-2^31, 2^31)` both give sections of `BigInt -> Int`; only the second preserves the signed order of `Int`. Such properties need their own checks."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/hom.md:156
msgid "A homomorphism trait `Hom[A, B]` implemented by types: it needs two type parameters, which MoonBit traits do not have, and it would allow only one homomorphism per pair of types, while a pair usually has several, such as the identity and conjugation on the complex numbers."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/hom.md:160
msgid "Plain functions `(A) -> B` without a certificate: nothing would separate checked homomorphisms from arbitrary conversions, and composition would not record where the obligations came from."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/hom.md:163
msgid "Treating representative lifts such as `Int -> BigInt` as homomorphisms: the cocycle argument above shows that they are not, so they get their own certificate, `Section`."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/hom.md:166
msgid "Recording the check relation (strict, lax, tolerance) in the type: it would multiply the inference rules for each strength. The relation is chosen at check time instead, and the boundaries below state the cost."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/hom.md:172
msgid "Only single-sorted signatures. Multi-sorted structures such as modules (scalars plus vectors) are out of scope for this subsystem."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/hom.md:174
msgid "Without higher-kinded types, functorial lifts (polynomials, matrices, ...) belong to their own packages and are not generalized here."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/hom.md:176
msgid "Laws are tested, not proven. The certificate guarantees traceable origin, not that the laws hold."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/hom.md:178
msgid "Runtime `Hom` endpoints carry `Model` identities, so `then_checked`, pairing, projections and strict upgrades reject different operation interpretations with `ModelMismatch`. `check_models` and `check_models_by` validate those endpoint identities and return `Result[Bool, ModelMismatch]`; the Algebra-only checks remain finite diagnostics over supplied dictionaries."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/hom.md:183
msgid "Model identity keys are opaque and package-owned. Callers compare Models with `Model::same_identity` and cannot inspect or mutate lineage paths."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/hom.md:185
msgid "The certificate does not record the relation used by `check_by`, so lax and approximate maps compose as if they were strict."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/hom.md:187
msgid "Fixed-width integers are ℤ/2^k, not ℤ or ℕ. Maps out of them into ℤ are sections, so they agree with the operations only while arithmetic does not wrap."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/hom.md:190
msgid "The section law does not fix which representatives are chosen, so properties such as order preservation need their own checks."
msgstr ""

